#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""EXAMPLE -- block a publish to a public destination when it would carry private data.

Fictional example for the reference tree. Adapt the term file and the tool
names to your client; do not run it unreviewed. CI runs its probe suite:
tests/pre-publish-scan-probes.py.example.

This is the FIRST of two layers. It runs before the agent's shell command, so
it sees a command string, not the push itself. A git `pre-push` hook is the
second layer: git runs it with the exact refs being pushed, after everything
earlier in the command has happened. Use both. See "Known limits" below.

Design notes worth keeping if you rewrite this:

  * It parses the command, it does not grep it. Commands are split into simple
    commands with quotes respected, following cd / pushd / popd, subshells,
    if/then and loops, `$(...)` even inside quotes, `bash -c` / `eval`
    strings, heredocs fed to a shell, `git -C`, xargs/find wrappers, an
    unquoted `$(...)` as part of its word (git -C $(pwd) push), backslash-newline
    line joins, a command name written in quotes (g'i't) or as zsh's =git, and
    git aliases (expanded, so an alias's own remote counts; one defined with
    `git -c alias.x=...` too, and one from GIT_CONFIG_*, --config-env or an
    included file, which it cannot read, blocks). A heredoc that is only
    data -- a note, a commit message -- is not read as commands, and neither is
    `git commit -m "... push ..."`. A word-matching gate blocks both of those,
    and a gate that blocks ordinary work gets switched off. The one exception:
    an UNQUOTED heredoc still runs its `$(...)` and backticks, so those are
    read as commands.

  * It tracks every directory the shell might be in. A cd that might not run
    -- inside an if, a loop, a case, a function or { } body, a pipeline, the
    background, a coproc, after `||`, after a step that could fail, or behind
    env / sudo / timeout, which run an external cd -- keeps the old directory
    as a candidate, and every candidate is scanned. A bare directory name
    counts as a cd (zsh's autocd option). Wrapper options that take a value
    (env -u NAME, nice -n 5, -C/dir glued on, or at the end of a cluster like
    -iC dir) are skipped with it, a wrapper named by its path (/usr/bin/env)
    counts, env -C / sudo -D move the command they run, env -S splits its
    string, and after an option the gate does not know it looks for git / gh
    / a shell further on. zsh's noglob / nocorrect / - are wrappers, and so is
    any other command (arch, stdbuf, script) whose arguments include git, gh
    or a shell -- no list of such programs is ever complete -- except ones that
    only print or compare (echo, grep, which, command -v, test, [[ ]]).
    Every such name in the
    arguments is followed, not just the first (find -name cd -exec git push). A
    quoted argument that names one is a script (watch 'git push', script -c
    'echo; git push'), and an option naming git with push further on counts as
    a push (rg --pre=git . push). A heredoc delimiter may be quoted with any
    characters ('END-OF-DATA') or by a backslash, and may hold a $ (EOF$x). A
    << inside
    quotes or a comment starts no heredoc. Text after # is read both as a
    comment and as commands, because which one it is depends on the shell; so
    a comment that names a publish can block in a dirty public repository, a
    deliberate backstop against a comment-parsing mistake. Scanning only the
    directory the cd names lets `if false; then cd clean-repo; fi; git push`
    publish the repository the shell never left.

  * The publishes it knows: git push (with --recurse-submodules=on-demand /
    only, or that setting, each submodule is scanned as a push of its own; its
    options are read as git reads them: --mirr is --mirror, -on is `-o n` and
    no dry run, the last of repeated options wins, and a positional repository
    beats --repo), and send-pack, subtree push and lfs push, which also send
    the repository's content; gh repo create / edit / rename / fork, gist
    create / edit / rename, release, api, label, project, discussion,
    agent-task create, workflow run, skill publish, and any issue or PR action
    that posts text (create, comment, close, merge...). gh's flags are read as gh reads them, from its own help (-pw is -p -w,
    --public=true, -R before the subcommand), and a gh alias -- set in the
    command or in gh's config -- is read as what it expands to.

  * It checks where each publish really goes: every push URL of the named
    remote, or for a bare push the branch's push remote; `--repo` for gh
    commands (GH_REPO or GH_HOST set blocks a gh publish that does not name its
    repository with --repo); a `gh api` endpoint's repository. An unresolved destination is
    public. `--dry-run` publishes nothing.

  * It scans what the publish can carry, not just what is on disk: file names
    and contents in the working tree (tracked and untracked; ignored files too
    when the command force-adds), symlink targets as text, the staged index,
    and every object the target remote does not have -- each blob whole, every
    file name in each tree, commit and tag messages, branch and tag names, and
    Git LFS objects. It reads objects, not `git log -p` output: a patch hides
    content added only by a merge commit, a line crafted to look like a diff
    header, and a tag that points straight at a blob. A bare repository has no
    working tree; its objects are scanned. What the remote already has is
    asked from the remote itself (git ls-remote): remote-tracking refs can be
    stale, and narrow the range only when the remote cannot be asked and the
    push URL is also the fetch URL. A remote with commits not fetched here
    excludes only what this clone has, so it can block until `git fetch`.
    Making a repository public, or creating one from a source directory, scans
    all history; creating one from a non-public template is blocked. Gist
    files, release assets, issue, PR, release and API text, closing and merge
    comments, a repository's description, topics and new name, and label
    names are scanned directly; an edited or renamed gist, and a project's
    text, whatever their visibility, which is not looked up (making a project
    public blocks). Text built by $(cat FILE) or $(< FILE) scans
    FILE, a heredoc inside $(...) is the text itself, and text that is any
    other command's output, stdin, or a shell variable blocks. A pager or
    editor setting (git -c core.pager=..., GIT_PAGER=..., man -P ...) is a
    command line and is read as one; man's page names are not.

  * It has no exclude list, deliberately. An exclusion added to quieten noise
    is where unreviewed content collects.

  * It FAILS CLOSED once a publish is involved. An unreadable or oversized
    file, an unresolvable repository or directory, a crash, a scan past its
    time budget, commands nested too deeply to follow, and content it cannot
    see (a body read from stdin, another repository's visibility change) all
    block. A crash is caught explicitly: most clients treat any exit code other
    than the blocking one as "carry on". A command that is not a publish is
    never blocked.

  * Its report names the file, line, and term-file line number only, and hides
    a file name that itself matches. Printing the matched text copies the
    protected value into logs and the conversation.

  * The term file lives OUTSIDE any published tree. A denylist committed to a
    public repository publishes the strings it exists to suppress. For
    generated output prefer an allowlist: assert every string present is one
    you intended to publish.

Known limits (why the pre-push layer exists):

  * Content created by an earlier step of the same command does not exist yet.
  * Shell is parsed, not run: a script file, a shell alias or function defined
    outside the command, or a command built from variables can hide a push. A
    directory or a git subcommand built from a variable blocks, and so does a
    command name built from one when `push` or another built word follows it;
    a script that pushes is not seen, nor is a gh command whose name is built
    from a variable. A gh extension is a program of its own, like a script.
  * Compressed formats (archives, most images) can hide text from a byte scan.
  * It scans every local branch and all untracked files, not only what this
    push sends. That is deliberate -- a script earlier in the command can
    commit anything -- so a private draft sitting in a public repository blocks
    until it is moved out or ignored.
  * Commit author, committer and tagger header lines are not scanned (a message
    line that merely starts with one of those words is): they carry the
    identity git is configured with, which is a question for that config, not
    for every push.

Hook payload arrives on stdin as JSON. Clients do not pass tool input via
environment variables.
"""

import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from pathlib import Path

TERMS_FILE = Path(__file__).parent / "private-terms.txt.example"
MAX_FILE_BYTES = 20_000_000
# Claude Code's PreToolUse default timeout is 600s and a timed-out gate now
# blocks, but it blocks with no reason. Our own budget says why.
try:  # the env override can only shorten the budget (probes use it)
    BUDGET = min(90.0, float(os.environ.get("PUBLISH_GATE_BUDGET", "90")))
except ValueError:
    BUDGET = 90.0
DEADLINE = time.monotonic() + BUDGET

# Every tool that runs a shell command. A client with a second one (a background
# runner, a monitor) must list it here, or it is an ungated shell.
SHELL_TOOLS = ("Bash",)

MAYBE_PUBLISH = re.compile(r"\b(?:git|gh|push)\b")  # push: a command name built by a variable
SEPARATOR = re.compile(r"^[;&|()\n]+$")
ASSIGNMENT = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*=")
REDIRECT = re.compile(r"^\d*(?:>>?|<<?<?-?|>\|)")
# The delimiter is one shell word, joined from quoted and bare pieces ('EOF'"-x", E"O"F, \EOF).
# It never starts with a digit, so the shift in $((1<<2)) starts no heredoc.
HEREDOC = re.compile(r"(?<!<)<<(-?)[ \t]*(?![0-9])((?:'[^'\n]*'|\"[^\"\n]*\"|\\[^\n]"
                     r"|[^\s;&|<>()'\"`\\])+)")
QUOTING = re.compile(r"'([^']*)'|\"([^\"]*)\"|\\(.)")
PLACEHOLDER = re.compile(r"\$\x02(\d+)\x03")  # an unquoted $(...) held out of tokenizing
HELD = []  # their scripts, by number
SHELL_WORD = re.compile(r"(?:^|[\s|;&(/])(?:bash|sh|zsh|dash|ksh|fish|eval|\$\{?SHELL\}?)(?=\s|$)")
REDIRECTING_CONFIG = re.compile(r"^(?:remote|url|branch|lfs|include|includeif)\.",
                                re.IGNORECASE)
ALIAS_SOURCE = re.compile(r"^(?:alias|include|includeif)\.", re.IGNORECASE)
GH_TARGET_ENV = re.compile(r"^(GH_REPO|GH_HOST)=(.*)", re.S)
REDIRECTING_ENV = re.compile(r"^GIT_(?:DIR|WORK_TREE|CONFIG\w*)=")
OPERATOR = re.compile(r"&&|\|\||;;&|;;|;&|\|&|[;&|\n()]")
CASE_END = (";;", ";&", ";;&")
KEYWORDS = {"if", "then", "else", "elif", "do", "while", "until", "!", "{", "coproc"}
OPENERS = {"if", "while", "until", "for", "select", "case", "{"}  # a body that may not run
CLOSERS = {"fi", "done", "esac", "}"}
CASE_WORD = re.compile(r"(?<![\w-])(?:case|esac)(?![\w-])")
IDENTITY = re.compile(r"^ ?(?:author|committer|tagger) ")
MAX_DEPTH = 4
MAX_DIRS = 8
BATCH_BYTES = 8_000_000
WRAPPERS = {"env", "command", "builtin", "exec", "nohup", "sudo", "nice", "caffeinate", "time",
            "noglob", "nocorrect", "-"}
IN_SHELL = {"command", "builtin", "time", "noglob", "nocorrect", "-"}
WRAPPER_VALUES = {  # options whose value is the next word, so that word is not the command
    "env": {"-u", "--unset", "-P", "-C", "--chdir", "-S", "--split-string"},
    "sudo": {"-u", "--user", "-g", "--group", "-C", "--close-from", "-D", "--chdir", "-h",
             "--host", "-p", "--prompt", "-r", "--role", "-t", "--type", "-U", "--other-user",
             "-T", "--command-timeout"},
    "nice": {"-n", "--adjustment"},
    "timeout": {"-s", "--signal", "-k", "--kill-after"},
    "caffeinate": {"-t", "-w"},
    "exec": {"-a"},
    "time": {"-f", "--format", "-o", "--output"},
}
WRAPPER_CHDIR = {"env": {"-C", "--chdir"}, "sudo": {"-D", "--chdir"}}
WRAPPER_FLAGS = {  # options known to take no value; anything else sends the gate searching
    "env": {"-", "-i", "-0", "-v", "--ignore-environment", "--null", "--debug"},
    "sudo": {"-A", "-B", "-b", "-E", "-e", "-H", "-i", "-K", "-k", "-l", "-N", "-n", "-P", "-S",
             "-s", "-V", "-v"},
    "timeout": {"-v", "--verbose", "--preserve-status", "--foreground"},
    "caffeinate": {"-d", "-i", "-m", "-s", "-u"},
    "exec": {"-c", "-l"},
    "time": {"-p", "-l", "-h", "-a", "-q", "-v"},
    "command": {"-p", "-v", "-V"},
}  # the rest run a separate process: its cd moves nothing
SHELLS = {"bash", "sh", "zsh", "dash"}
RUNS_COMMAND = re.compile(r"^(?:\w*_)?(?:PAGER|MANPAGER|EDITOR|VISUAL|BROWSER|SSH_COMMAND)$")
UNFETCHED = []  # repositories whose remote has commits not fetched here
DYNAMIC = re.compile(r"[$`{}]")  # a command name built by a variable, substitution or escape
FANOUT = {"xargs", "find", "parallel", "watch"}
HANDLED = {"cd", "pushd", "popd", "eval", "git", "gh"} | SHELLS | FANOUT
# Commands known to never run their arguments. Any other command whose arguments include git, gh
# or a shell is read as running it: arch, stdbuf, script and the like have no list that is ever
# complete, and a word missing HERE costs a false block, never a missed push.
PRINTS_ONLY = {"echo", "printf", "print", "type", "which", "whence", "where", "grep", "egrep",
               "fgrep", "rg", "test", "[", "[["}
GIT_BUILTINS = set("""
add am annotate apply archive bisect blame branch bundle cat-file check-attr check-ignore
check-ref-format checkout checkout-index cherry cherry-pick citool clean clone column commit
commit-graph commit-tree config count-objects credential describe diff diff-files diff-index
diff-tree difftool fast-export fast-import fetch fetch-pack filter-branch fmt-merge-msg
for-each-ref for-each-repo format-patch fsck gc get-tar-commit-id grep gui hash-object help
hook index-pack init instaweb interpret-trailers log ls-files ls-remote ls-tree mailinfo
mailsplit maintenance merge merge-base merge-file merge-tree mktag mktree multi-pack-index mv
name-rev notes pack-objects pack-refs patch-id prune prune-packed pull range-diff read-tree
rebase reflog remote repack replace request-pull rerere reset restore rev-list rev-parse revert
rm send-email shortlog show show-branch show-ref sparse-checkout stash status stripspace
submodule switch symbolic-ref tag unpack-file unpack-objects update-index update-ref
update-server-info var verify-commit verify-pack verify-tag version whatchanged worktree
write-tree lfs subtree send-pack
""".split())
GITHUB_REPO = re.compile(r"github\.com[:/]+([^/\s]+)/([^/\s]+?)(?:\.git)?/?$", re.IGNORECASE)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- parsing

class Parsed:
    def __init__(self):
        self.ops = []            # (kind, dirs, args)
        self.force_add = False
        self.env_config = False  # GIT_CONFIG_* set: git aliases may come from the environment
        self.gh_env = None  # GH_REPO / GH_HOST set: gh's repository is not the one here
        self.gh_aliases = {}  # gh alias set earlier in this command
        self.gh_alias_opaque = False  # gh aliases from somewhere this gate does not read
        for key in ("GH_REPO", "GH_HOST"):
            gh_target(f"{key}={os.environ.get(key, '')}", self)
        self.redirected = False  # git --git-dir / --work-tree
        self.pushd = []


def strip_heredocs(command, comments=False):
    """Drop heredoc bodies that are data (a note, a commit message) so prose mentioning a
    push is not read as one -- a note written this way was falsely blocked. A body fed
    to a shell (`bash <<EOF`, `cat <<EOF | sh`) is a script and stays. An unquoted delimiter
    still runs the body's $(...) and `...` before the data reaches its reader, so those stay as
    subshells. Only a << outside quotes (and, when `comments`, outside a comment) starts a
    heredoc: `echo '<<EOF'` does not, and the lines after it run. Unterminated: keep all."""
    out, pos, i, quote = [], 0, 0, None
    while i < len(command):
        ch = command[i]
        if ch == "\\" and quote != "'":
            i += 2
            continue
        if quote or ch in "'\"":
            quote = None if ch == quote else quote or ch
            i += 1
            continue
        if comments and ch == "#" and (i == 0 or command[i - 1] in " \t\n;&|()"):
            i = command.find("\n", i) if "\n" in command[i:] else len(command)
            continue
        m = HEREDOC.match(command, i)
        if not m:
            i += 1
            continue
        line_start = command.rfind("\n", 0, m.start()) + 1
        line_end = command.find("\n", m.end())
        if line_end == -1:
            break
        indent = r"[ \t]*" if m.group(1) else ""
        delimiter = QUOTING.sub(lambda q: next(g for g in q.groups() if g is not None),
                                m.group(2))
        quoted = delimiter != m.group(2)  # any quoting makes the body literal
        end = re.compile(rf"^{indent}{re.escape(delimiter)}[ \t]*$", re.M).search(
            command, line_end + 1)
        if not end:
            break
        out.append(command[pos:line_end + 1])
        if SHELL_WORD.search(command[line_start:line_end]):
            out.append(command[line_end + 1:end.end()])
        elif not quoted:
            out.extend(f"({script})\n" for script in
                       substitutions(command[line_end + 1:end.start()]))
        pos = i = end.end()
    out.append(command[pos:])
    return "".join(out)


def strip_comments(command):
    """Drop `# ...` to the end of the line where a shell word starts with #. Quotes and
    backslashes keep a # literal, and so does # inside a word (a#b)."""
    out, i, quote = [], 0, None
    while i < len(command):
        ch = command[i]
        if ch == "\\" and quote != "'":
            out.append(command[i:i + 2])
            i += 2
            continue
        if quote:
            quote = None if ch == quote else quote
        elif ch in "'\"":
            quote = ch
        elif ch == "#" and (i == 0 or command[i - 1] in " \t\n;&|()"):
            end = command.find("\n", i)
            i = len(command) if end == -1 else end
            continue
        out.append(ch)
        i += 1
    return "".join(out)


def inert_single_quotes(command):
    """Single quotes make $( and ` plain text. Tokenizing drops the quotes, so mark those first
    (as \0 and \1) or `echo '$(git push)'` reads as a push; any other $ there, and an
    escaped \\$ anywhere, becomes \4, so '$5' and "\\$HOME" are not taken for variables. Outside single quotes a
    backslash-newline joins two lines into one, so `git \\<newline> push` is one command."""
    out, i, quote = [], 0, None
    while i < len(command):
        ch = command[i]
        if ch == "\\" and quote != "'":
            if command.startswith("\n", i + 1):
                i += 2
                continue
            out.append("\4" if command.startswith("$", i + 1) else command[i:i + 2])
            i += 2
            continue
        if quote == "'" and ch == "`":
            ch = "\1"
        elif quote == "'" and command.startswith("$(", i):
            ch = "\0"
        elif quote == "'" and ch == "$":
            ch = "\4"  # literal, not a variable
        elif quote:
            quote = None if ch == quote else quote
        elif ch in "'\"":
            quote = ch
        out.append(ch)
        i += 1
    return "".join(out)


def hold_substitutions(command):
    """An unquoted $(...) or `...` is part of the word around it: `git -C $(pwd) push` is one
    command. Tokenizing would split it at its parentheses or spaces and cut the command in two,
    so each is swapped for a placeholder word first; substitutions() hands its script back. One
    inside double quotes already stays in its token and is left alone."""
    out, i, quote = [], 0, None
    while i < len(command):
        ch = command[i]
        if ch == "\\" and quote != "'":
            out.append(command[i:i + 2])
            i += 2
            continue
        if quote != "'" and (command.startswith("$(", i) or ch == "`"):
            found = substitutions(command[i:])
            end = i + (2 if ch == "$" else 1) + len(found[0]) + 1 if found else 0
            if found and command[end - 1:end] == (")" if ch == "$" else "`"):
                if quote:
                    out.append(command[i:end])
                else:
                    HELD.append(found[0])
                    out.append(f"$\x02{len(HELD) - 1}\x03")
                i = end
                continue
        if ch in "'\"" and quote in (None, ch):
            quote = None if quote else ch
        out.append(ch)
        i += 1
    return "".join(out)


def tokenize(command, comments=False):
    command = strip_heredocs(command, comments)
    if comments:
        command = strip_comments(command)
    command = hold_substitutions(inert_single_quotes(command))
    try:
        lex = shlex.shlex(command, posix=True, punctuation_chars=";&|()\n")
        lex.whitespace = " \t\r"
        lex.whitespace_split = True
        lex.commenters = ""
        return list(lex)
    except ValueError:  # unbalanced quote (often heredoc prose): split crudely
        return re.findall(r"[;&|()\n]+|[^\s;&|()]+", command)


def merge(*options):
    """Every directory the shell may be in. Unknown anywhere is unknown; so is a list too long
    to keep, because dropping a candidate would drop the directory the shell is really in."""
    if any(o is None for o in options):
        return None
    out = tuple(dict.fromkeys(d for o in options for d in o))
    return out if len(out) <= MAX_DIRS else None


def change_dir(dirs, target):
    """A cd target may not exist yet, or may fail; keep the old dir as a candidate."""
    if dirs is None or target == "-" or re.search(r"[$`*?]", target) \
            or re.match(r"^[+-]\d+$", target):  # a directory-stack entry
        return None
    out = []
    for d in dirs:
        new = os.path.normpath(os.path.join(d, os.path.expanduser(target)))
        out.append(new)
        if not (os.path.isdir(new) and os.access(new, os.X_OK)):
            out.append(d)
    return merge(out)


def strip_redirects(cmd):
    out, skip = [], False
    for tok in cmd:
        if skip:
            skip = False
            continue
        m = REDIRECT.match(tok)
        if m and not re.search(r"\s", tok):
            skip = m.end() == len(tok)
            continue
        out.append(tok)
    return out


def substitutions(token):
    """The scripts inside $(...) and `...` in one token. A quoted substitution stays a single
    token after tokenizing, but the shell still runs it."""
    out, i = [], 0
    while i < len(token):
        held = PLACEHOLDER.match(token, i)
        if held:
            out.append(HELD[int(held.group(1))])
            i = held.end()
        elif token.startswith("$(", i):
            depth, j, quote, cases = 1, i + 2, None, 0
            while j < len(token) and depth:
                ch = token[j]
                word = CASE_WORD.match(token, j)
                if ch == "\\" and quote != "'":  # an escaped ) or quote is just a character
                    j += 2
                    continue
                if quote:
                    quote = None if ch == quote else quote
                elif ch in "'\"":
                    quote = ch
                elif word:
                    cases += 1 if word.group() == "case" else -1 if cases else 0
                    j = word.end() - 1
                elif not cases:  # inside a case, `pattern)` is not a closing paren
                    depth += {"(": 1, ")": -1}.get(ch, 0)
                j += 1
            out.append(token[i + 2:j - 1])
            i = j
        elif token[i] == "`":
            j = i + 1
            while j < len(token) and token[j] != "`":
                j += 2 if token[j] == "\\" else 1
            if j >= len(token):
                break
            out.append(token[i + 1:j])
            i = j + 1
        else:
            i += 1
    return out


CASE = object()


def walk(tokens, dirs, parsed, depth=0):
    """Returns the directories the shell may be in afterwards. A directory change is certain
    only when it runs unconditionally: not after || or inside a pipeline, the background, or an
    if / loop / case / { } body (a function body is one). Otherwise the old directory stays a
    candidate. After `a && cd x`, x holds for the rest of that && chain; once the chain ends,
    the directory from before any step that could have stopped it stays a candidate too."""
    stack, cmd = [], []
    cond, before = 0, ";"
    start, stops = dirs, ()  # where this and-or list began; where it may have stopped early
    for tok in tokens + [";"]:
        if not SEPARATOR.match(tok):
            for script in substitutions(tok):
                nested(script, dirs, parsed, depth)
            cmd.append(tok)
            continue
        ops = OPERATOR.findall(tok)
        if cmd:
            detached, i = False, 0
            while i < len(cmd):
                word = cmd[i]
                if word == "function":  # `function name { ... }`
                    i += 2
                    continue
                if word == "case":
                    stack.append(CASE)  # its `pattern)` closes no subshell
                if word in OPENERS:
                    cond += 1
                elif word in CLOSERS:
                    cond = max(0, cond - 1)
                    if word == "esac" and stack and stack[-1] is CASE:
                        stack.pop()
                elif word == "coproc":
                    detached = True
                elif word not in KEYWORDS:
                    break
                i += 1
            new = simple_command(cmd, dirs, parsed, depth)
            if new != dirs:
                after = ops[0] if ops else ";"
                if before == "&&":
                    stops = merge(stops, dirs)
                if cond or detached or before in ("||", "|", "|&") \
                        or after in ("|", "|&", "&"):
                    new = merge(dirs, new)
                dirs = new
            cmd = []
        for op in ops:
            if op == "(":
                stack.append((dirs, start, stops, cond))
                start, stops, before = dirs, (), ";"
            elif op == ")":
                if stack and stack[-1] is not CASE:
                    dirs, start, stops, cond = stack.pop()
                before = ";"
            elif op in (";", "\n", "&") + CASE_END:
                dirs = merge(dirs, stops)
                start, stops, before = dirs, (), ";"
            elif op == "||":
                dirs = merge(start, stops, dirs)
                before = op
            else:
                before = op
    return dirs


def nested(script, dirs, parsed, depth):
    """Parse a script the shell will run: a substitution, a -c string, eval, an alias. Past
    the depth limit, block instead of silently stopping. Returns the directories afterwards,
    which only eval keeps (it runs in this shell)."""
    if depth >= MAX_DEPTH:
        if MAYBE_PUBLISH.search(script):
            parsed.ops.append(("unseen", dirs, ["commands nested too deeply to check"]))
        return None
    return walk(tokenize(script), dirs, parsed, depth + 1)


def simple_command(cmd, dirs, parsed, depth):
    cmd = strip_redirects(cmd)
    shell_dirs = dirs  # a wrapper's chdir moves only the command it runs
    i, external, wrapped, unknown = 0, False, False, False
    while i < len(cmd):
        if ASSIGNMENT.match(cmd[i]) or cmd[i] in KEYWORDS:
            runs_command(cmd[i], dirs, parsed, depth)
            if REDIRECTING_ENV.match(cmd[i]):
                parsed.redirected = True
            parsed.env_config = parsed.env_config or cmd[i].startswith("GIT_CONFIG")
            gh_target(cmd[i], parsed)
            i += 1
        elif cmd[i] == "function":
            i += 2
        elif command_name(cmd[i]) in WRAPPERS | {"timeout"}:
            wrapper, wrapped = command_name(cmd[i]), True
            external = external or wrapper not in IN_SHELL
            i += 1
            while i < len(cmd) and cmd[i].startswith("-"):
                if cmd[i] == "--":
                    i += 1
                    break
                opt, value, i, known = wrapper_option(wrapper, cmd, i)
                unknown = unknown or not known
                if wrapper == "command" and not opt.startswith("--") and set(opt) & set("vV"):
                    return shell_dirs  # command -v / -V only names the command
                if opt in WRAPPER_CHDIR.get(wrapper, ()):
                    dirs = change_dir(dirs, value)
                elif wrapper == "env" and opt in ("-S", "--split-string"):
                    nested(" ".join([value] + cmd[i:]), dirs, parsed, depth)
                    return shell_dirs
            if wrapper == "timeout":
                i += 1  # the duration
        else:
            break
    cmd = cmd[i:]
    if not cmd:
        return shell_dirs
    word, args = command_name(cmd[0]), cmd[1:]
    if DYNAMIC.search(word) and ("push" in args or args and DYNAMIC.search(args[0])
                                 and "/" not in args[0]):  # "$g" "$p": maybe git push
        parsed.ops.append(("unseen", dirs, ["a command name built by a variable or "
                                            "substitution, with push or a built subcommand"]))
        return shell_dirs
    if word in ("export", "declare", "typeset"):
        for a in args:
            runs_command(a, dirs, parsed, depth)
            gh_target(a, parsed)
            if REDIRECTING_ENV.match(a):
                parsed.redirected = True  # every later git uses it, as a prefix would
    if word in ("export", "declare", "typeset") and any(a.startswith("GIT_CONFIG") for a in args):
        parsed.env_config = True  # an alias defined there applies to every later git
    if wrapped and unknown and word not in HANDLED:
        # An option this gate does not know may have taken a value: find the command anyway.
        run_arguments(cmd[1:], dirs, parsed, depth)
        return shell_dirs

    if word in ("cd", "pushd", "popd", "eval"):
        moved = move(word, args, dirs, parsed, depth)
        return merge(shell_dirs, moved) if external else moved
    if not args and not external and is_autocd(cmd[0], dirs):
        return merge(dirs, change_dir(dirs, cmd[0]))  # bash has no autocd: it may stay put
    if word in SHELLS:
        for j, a in enumerate(args):
            if re.match(r"^-[a-z]*c[a-z]*$", a) and j + 1 < len(args):
                nested(args[j + 1], dirs, parsed, depth)
                break
    elif word == "git":
        git_command(args, dirs, parsed, depth)
    elif word == "gh":
        gh_command(args, dirs, parsed, depth)
    elif word == "man":  # page names are data; -P / -H name a command it runs
        for k, a in enumerate(args):
            for opt in ("--pager", "--html", "-P", "-H"):
                if a == opt and k + 1 < len(args):
                    nested(args[k + 1], dirs, parsed, depth)
                elif a.startswith(opt) and a != opt:
                    nested(a[len(opt):].lstrip("="), dirs, parsed, depth)
                    break
    elif word in FANOUT or word not in HANDLED | PRINTS_ONLY \
            or word == "rg" and any(a.startswith("--pre") for a in args):  # rg --pre runs one
        run_arguments(args, dirs, parsed, depth)
    return shell_dirs


def run_arguments(args, dirs, parsed, depth):
    """Read the arguments of a command that may run them. Every word naming git, gh or a
    shell may start the command it runs (an earlier one may be a file name or a value); a single quoted word holding several words that name one
    anywhere (watch 'git push', script -c 'echo; git push', flock f -c '...', man -P '...') is
    a script of its own."""
    for k, a in enumerate(args):
        named = a.partition("=")[2] if a.startswith("-") else a
        if command_name(named) == "git" and "push" in args[k + 1:]:
            # it may add words of its own: rg --pre=git . push runs `git push`
            parsed.ops.append(("push", dirs, []))
        if DYNAMIC.search(command_name(named)) and re.search(r"git|gh", named, re.I) \
                and "push" in args[k + 1:]:
            parsed.ops.append(("unseen", dirs, ["a push whose command name is built by a "
                                                "variable or substitution"]))
        if command_name(a) in HANDLED:  # and keep looking: find -name cd -exec git push
            simple_command(args[k:], dirs, parsed, depth)
        if len(a.split()) > 1 and MAYBE_PUBLISH.search(a):
            nested(a, dirs, parsed, depth)


def runs_command(assignment, dirs, parsed, depth):
    """PAGER=, GIT_PAGER=, MANPAGER=, EDITOR=, VISUAL=, BROWSER=, GIT_SSH_COMMAND= name a
    command line the program runs: read it as one."""
    name, _, value = assignment.partition("=")
    if RUNS_COMMAND.match(name) and MAYBE_PUBLISH.search(value):
        nested(value, dirs, parsed, depth)


def command_name(word):
    """The program a command word names. zsh expands `=git` to git's path (EQUALS option)."""
    return os.path.basename(word[1:] if word.startswith("=") else word)


def wrapper_option(wrapper, cmd, i):
    """(option, value, next index, recognised) for the option word at cmd[i]. A value may be
    the next word, glued on (-C/dir, -S'git push'), or end a short-option cluster (-iC dir)."""
    tok = cmd[i]
    values, flags = WRAPPER_VALUES.get(wrapper, set()), WRAPPER_FLAGS.get(wrapper, set())
    following = cmd[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(cmd) else ""
    if tok.startswith("--"):
        opt, eq, value = tok.partition("=")
        if opt in values:
            return (opt, value, i + 1, True) if eq else (opt, following, i + 2, True)
        return opt, value, i + 1, opt in flags
    known = True
    for k in range(1, len(tok)):
        opt = "-" + tok[k]
        if opt in values:
            rest = tok[k + 1:]
            return (opt, rest, i + 1, known) if rest else (opt, following, i + 2, known)
        known = known and opt in flags
    return tok, "", i + 1, known if len(tok) > 1 else tok in flags


def is_autocd(word, dirs):
    """zsh's autocd: a bare directory name that is not a command is a cd."""
    return bool(dirs) and shutil.which(word) is None and any(
        os.path.isdir(os.path.join(d, os.path.expanduser(word))) for d in dirs)


def move(word, args, dirs, parsed, depth):
    """The directories after cd / pushd / popd / eval."""
    if word == "eval":
        return nested(" ".join(args), dirs, parsed, depth)
    if word == "popd":
        if args:  # popd +N / -N drops an entry the stack model does not track
            return None
        return parsed.pushd.pop() if parsed.pushd else None
    targets, options = [], True
    for a in args:
        if options and a == "--":
            options = False
        elif not (options and a.startswith("-") and a != "-" and not re.match(r"^[+-]\d+$", a)):
            targets.append(a)
    if word == "pushd":
        parsed.pushd.append(dirs)
    if len(targets) > 1 or (word == "pushd" and not targets):
        return None  # zsh `cd old new` rewrites the path; a bare pushd swaps the stack
    return change_dir(dirs, targets[0] if targets else "~")


def git_command(args, dirs, parsed, depth):
    i, inline, opaque, recurse = 0, {}, parsed.env_config, []
    while i < len(args) and args[i].startswith("-"):
        a = args[i]
        if a == "-C":
            dirs = change_dir(dirs, args[i + 1]) if i + 1 < len(args) else None
            i += 2
        elif a in ("-c", "--config-env") or a.startswith("--config-env="):
            glued = a.startswith("--config-env=")
            setting = a.partition("=")[2] if glued else args[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(args) else ""
            if REDIRECTING_CONFIG.match(setting):
                parsed.redirected = True  # e.g. -c remote.origin.url=...: a destination we
            key, _, value = setting.partition("=")  # cannot see through `git remote`
            if len(value.split()) > 1 and MAYBE_PUBLISH.search(value):
                nested(value.lstrip("!"), dirs, parsed, depth)  # core.pager, core.editor...
            if key.lower() == "push.recursesubmodules":
                recurse = [f"--recurse-submodules={value}"]  # before the command's own flags
            if a == "-c" and key.lower().startswith("alias."):
                inline[key[6:].lower()] = value
            elif ALIAS_SOURCE.match(key):
                opaque = True  # an alias from an environment variable or an included file
            i += 1 if glued else 2
        elif a == "--namespace":
            i += 2
        elif a.split("=")[0] in ("--git-dir", "--work-tree"):
            parsed.redirected = True
            i += 1 if "=" in a else 2
        else:
            i += 1
    if i >= len(args):
        return
    sub, rest = args[i], args[i + 1:]
    if re.search(r"[$`]", sub):
        parsed.ops.append(("unseen", dirs, ["a git subcommand built from a variable"]))
    elif sub in ("push", "send-pack"):  # send-pack: the plumbing behind push (a URL, refs)
        opts, words = push_args(recurse + rest)
        if not dry_run(opts):
            parsed.ops.append(("push", dirs, opts + ["--"] + words))
    elif sub in ("subtree", "lfs") and "push" in rest:
        # Both send to <repository>; what they send comes from this repo's history and LFS
        # store, which a push scan covers whole when given no refspec.
        words = positionals(rest, {"-P", "--prefix", "-b", "--branch", "--onto", "--annotate",
                                   "-m", "--message"})
        if (sub == "subtree" or "--dry-run" not in rest) and "push" in words:  # subtree has none
            k = words.index("push")
            parsed.ops.append(("push", dirs, words[k + 1:k + 2]))
    elif sub == "add":
        if any(r.startswith("--f") and "--force".startswith(r) or re.match(r"^-[a-zA-Z]*f", r)
               for r in rest):  # --forc: git takes any unique prefix of an option
            parsed.force_add = True
    elif sub == "submodule" and "push" in rest:
        parsed.ops.append(("unseen", dirs, ["a push inside `git submodule foreach`"]))
    elif sub.lower() in inline:
        alias = inline[sub.lower()]
        script = alias[1:] if alias.startswith("!") else "git " + alias
        nested(script + " " + shlex.join(rest), dirs, parsed, depth)
    elif sub not in GIT_BUILTINS and opaque:
        parsed.ops.append(("unseen", dirs, ["a git alias defined where this gate cannot read "
                                            "it (GIT_CONFIG_*, --config-env, include)"]))
    elif sub not in GIT_BUILTINS and dirs:
        # Expand the alias itself: `!git push public main` carries its own destination.
        for d in existing(dirs):
            alias = run(["git", "config", "--get", f"alias.{sub}"], d)
            if alias:
                script = alias[1:] if alias.startswith("!") else "git " + alias
                nested(script + " " + shlex.join(rest), (d,), parsed, depth)


def gh_target(word, parsed):
    """Note a GH_REPO / GH_HOST assignment: it applies to this gh and every later one."""
    if re.match(r"(GH_CONFIG_DIR|XDG_CONFIG_HOME)=", word):
        parsed.gh_alias_opaque = True  # gh's aliases then come from another config
    m = GH_TARGET_ENV.match(word)
    if m and m.group(2) and not (m.group(1) == "GH_HOST" and m.group(2) == "github.com"):
        parsed.gh_env = "GH_HOST" if "GH_HOST" in (m.group(1), parsed.gh_env) else "GH_REPO"


GH_GROUPS = {"repo", "gist", "release", "issue", "pr", "api", "discussion", "label", "project",
             "agent-task", "workflow", "skill", "alias"}  # gh's own: never an alias
GH_FLAG_LINE = re.compile(r"^\s+(?:-(\S), )?--([\w-]+)(?: (\S+))?(?:\s{2,}|$)")
PFLAG_FALSE = {"0", "f", "F", "false", "FALSE", "False"}
_gh_flags = {}
_gh_aliases = []


def gh_flags(command):
    """{flag: (long name, takes a value)} for one of gh's own commands, read from that gh's
    help (inherited flags included), or None when it cannot be read. Never asked of an alias
    or an extension: `gh <that> --help` would run it."""
    key = tuple(command)
    if key not in _gh_flags:
        table = {}
        for line in (run(["gh", *command, "--help"], None) or "").splitlines():
            m = GH_FLAG_LINE.match(line)
            if m:
                entry = ("--" + m.group(2), m.group(3) is not None)
                table[entry[0]] = entry
                if m.group(1):
                    table["-" + m.group(1)] = entry
        _gh_flags[key] = table or None
    return _gh_flags[key]


def gh_args(args, table):
    """A gh command line read as gh's own flag parser (pflag) reads it: bundled short flags
    (-pw), a value glued (-dText, -d=Text) or in the next word, a bool given a value
    (--public=true; --public=false is dropped). Each flag comes back as one word, --NAME or
    --NAME=VALUE, ahead of the positional words. A flag this gh does not know is kept as
    written (gh refuses it). Without a table the words come back unchanged."""
    if not table:
        return list(args)
    flags, words, i = [], [], 0
    while i < len(args):
        a, i = args[i], i + 1
        if a == "--":
            words += args[i - 1:]
            break
        if a.startswith("--"):
            name, eq, value = a.partition("=")
            long, takes = table.get(name, (None, None))
            if long is None:
                flags.append(a)
            elif not takes:
                if not eq or value not in PFLAG_FALSE:
                    flags.append(long)
            else:
                if not eq and i < len(args):
                    value, i = args[i], i + 1
                flags.append(f"{long}={value}")
        elif a.startswith("-") and len(a) > 1:
            k = 1
            while k < len(a):
                long, takes = table.get("-" + a[k], (None, None))
                if long is None:
                    flags.append("-" + a[k:])
                    break
                glued, k = a[k + 1:], k + 1
                if takes:
                    value = glued[1:] if glued.startswith("=") else glued
                    if not glued and i < len(args):
                        value, i = args[i], i + 1
                    flags.append(f"{long}={value}")
                    break
                if glued.startswith("="):  # -p=false
                    if glued[1:] not in PFLAG_FALSE:
                        flags.append(long)
                    break
                flags.append(long)
        else:
            words.append(a)
    return flags + words


def split_action(args, table):
    """(subcommand, the other words): a flag of the group may come first, as in
    gh issue -R owner/repo create."""
    i = 0
    while i < len(args) and args[i] != "--":
        a = args[i]
        if not a.startswith("-") or a == "-":
            return a, args[:i] + args[i + 1:]
        _, takes = (table or {}).get(a.partition("=")[0] if a.startswith("--") else a[:2],
                                     (None, False))
        i += 2 if takes and "=" not in a and (a.startswith("--") or len(a) == 2) else 1
    return "", list(args)


def gh_alias_list():
    """gh's configured aliases ({name: expansion}; None where it could not be read). The list
    is YAML: a value that needs it is quoted."""
    if not _gh_aliases:
        found = {}
        for line in (run(["gh", "alias", "list"], None) or "").splitlines():
            name, sep, value = line.partition(": ")
            if not sep:
                continue
            if value[:1] == "'" and value[-1:] == "'" and len(value) > 1:
                value = value[1:-1].replace("''", "'")
            elif value[:1] == '"':
                try:
                    value = json.loads(value)
                except ValueError:
                    value = None
            found[name] = value
        _gh_aliases.append(found)
    return _gh_aliases[0]


def gh_alias(args, dirs, parsed, depth):
    """gh <alias>: one set earlier in this command, or in gh's config, is walked as what it
    expands to (a ! alias through the shell). Anything else is an extension, a program of its
    own like any script, or one of gh's reads."""
    name = args[0] if args else ""
    known = parsed.gh_aliases if name in parsed.gh_aliases else gh_alias_list()
    if name not in known:
        if parsed.gh_alias_opaque:
            parsed.ops.append(("unseen", dirs, ["a gh alias this gate cannot read"]))
        return
    expansion = known[name]
    if expansion is None:
        parsed.ops.append(("unseen", dirs, ["a gh alias this gate cannot read"]))
    elif expansion.startswith("!"):
        nested(expansion[1:], dirs, parsed, depth)  # its arguments are $1, $2...: unseen text
    else:
        extra = []
        for n, a in enumerate(args[1:], 1):  # as gh expands: $N replaced, the rest appended
            if "$" in expansion:
                expansion = expansion.replace(f"${n}", a)
            else:
                extra.append(a)
        try:
            nested(shlex.join(["gh", *shlex.split(expansion), *extra]), dirs, parsed, depth)
        except ValueError:
            parsed.ops.append(("unseen", dirs, ["a gh alias this gate cannot read"]))


def gh_command(args, dirs, parsed, depth=0):
    group = args[0] if args else ""
    if group not in GH_GROUPS:
        gh_alias(args, dirs, parsed, depth)
        return
    if group == "api":
        action, rest = "", gh_args(args[1:], gh_flags(["api"]))
    else:
        table = gh_flags([group])
        if table is None and args[1:2] and args[1].startswith("-"):
            parsed.ops.append(("unseen", dirs, ["gh flags this gate could not read"]))
            return
        action, rest = split_action(args[1:], table)
        rest = gh_args(rest, gh_flags([group, action]) if action else None)
    before = len(parsed.ops)
    gh_ops(group, action, rest, dirs, parsed)
    if parsed.gh_env and len(parsed.ops) > before \
            and (parsed.gh_env == "GH_HOST" or not flag_values(rest, "--repo", "-R")):
        del parsed.ops[before:]
        parsed.ops.append(("unseen", dirs, [f"{parsed.gh_env} is set, so gh may publish to a "
                                            "repository this gate does not resolve (name it "
                                            "with --repo instead)"]))


def gh_ops(group, action, rest, dirs, parsed):
    repo = (flag_values(rest, "--repo", "-R") or [None])[0]

    def text(texts, target=repo, files=(), kind="text"):
        if texts or files:
            parsed.ops.append((kind, dirs, [*([f"--repo={target}"] if target else []),
                                            *(f"--body={t}" for t in texts),
                                            *(f"--body-file={f}" for f in files)]))

    words = positionals(rest, {"--repo", "-R"})
    if group == "repo" and action == "create":
        parsed.ops.append(("repo-create", dirs, rest))
    elif group == "repo" and action == "edit":
        if "public" in (v.lower() for v in flag_values(rest, "--visibility")):
            parsed.ops.append(("make-public", dirs, rest))
        named = positionals(rest, REPO_EDIT_VALUES)[:1]
        # shown on the repository's page
        text(flag_values(rest, "--description", "-d", "--homepage", "-h", "--add-topic"),
             named[0] if named else None)
    elif group == "repo" and action == "rename":  # the new name
        text(words[:1])
    elif group == "repo" and action == "fork":  # a fork of a public repository is public
        text(flag_values(rest, "--fork-name"), (words or [None])[0])
    elif group == "gist" and action == "create":
        parsed.ops.append(("gist", dirs, rest))
    elif group == "gist" and action == "edit":
        parsed.ops.append(("gist-edit", dirs, rest))
    elif group == "gist" and action == "rename":  # an existing gist: visibility not looked up
        text(words[2:3], kind="text-anywhere")
    elif group == "release" and action in ("create", "upload", "edit"):
        parsed.ops.append(("release-" + action, dirs, rest))
    elif group in ("issue", "pr", "discussion") and action not in GH_READS:
        parsed.ops.append(("text", dirs, rest))  # close / reopen / merge post text too
        if group == "pr" and action == "create" and not flag_values(rest, "--head", "-H"):
            parsed.ops.append(("push", dirs, []))  # gh may push the branch first
    elif group == "label" and action in ("create", "edit"):
        text(words[:1] + flag_values(rest, "--description", "-d", "--name", "-n"))
    elif group == "label" and action == "clone":
        parsed.ops.append(("label-clone", dirs, rest))
    elif group == "project":  # a project's visibility is not looked up: its text always counts
        if "public" in (v.lower() for v in flag_values(rest, "--visibility")):
            parsed.ops.append(("unseen", dirs, ["a project made public (its items are not "
                                                "scanned)"]))
        text(flag_values(rest, "--title", "--description", "-d", "--readme", "--body", "--text",
                         "--value", "--name", "--single-select-options"), kind="text-anywhere")
    elif group == "agent-task" and action == "create":  # the task and its pull request
        text(words[:1], files=flag_values(rest, "--from-file", "-F"))
    elif group == "workflow" and action == "run":  # inputs of a run in a public repository
        fields = [f.partition("=")[2] for f in flag_values(rest, "-f", "--raw-field", "-F",
                                                              "--field")]
        text([f for f in fields if not f.startswith("@")],
             files=[f[1:] for f in fields if f.startswith("@")]
             + (["-"] if "--json" in rest else []))
    elif group == "skill" and action == "publish" and "--dry-run" not in rest:
        parsed.ops.append(("push", dirs, []))  # a release of this repository's skills
    elif group == "alias" and action == "set":
        named = positionals(rest, set())
        if len(named) >= 2 and named[1] != "-":
            parsed.gh_aliases[named[0]] = ("!" if "--shell" in rest else "") + named[1]
        else:
            parsed.gh_alias_opaque = True
    elif group == "alias" and action == "import":
        parsed.gh_alias_opaque = True
    elif group == "api":
        parsed.ops.append(("api", dirs, rest))


# ------------------------------------------------------------ arguments

GH_READS = {"", "list", "view", "status", "checks", "diff", "checkout", "develop"}
REPO_EDIT_VALUES = {"--visibility", "--description", "-d", "--homepage", "-h", "--default-branch",
                    "--add-topic", "--remove-topic", "--squash-merge-commit-message"}


def flag_values(args, *names):
    out = []
    for j, a in enumerate(args):
        for name in names:
            if a == name and j + 1 < len(args):
                out.append(args[j + 1])
            elif name.startswith("--") and a.startswith(name + "="):
                out.append(a[len(name) + 1:])
            elif len(name) == 2 and a.startswith(name) and len(a) > 2:  # -fVALUE
                out.append(a[2:])
    return out


PUSH_LONG = ("--repo", "--receive-pack", "--exec", "--push-option", "--recurse-submodules",
             "--remote", "--no-recurse-submodules", "--mirror", "--stdin", "--dry-run",
             "--no-dry-run")
PUSH_VALUED = {"--repo", "--receive-pack", "--exec", "--push-option", "--recurse-submodules",
               "--remote"}  # every push / send-pack option whose value may be the next word


def push_args(args):
    """A push / send-pack command line read as git's own option parser reads it: any unique
    prefix of a long option (--mirr, --rep=URL), a value in the next word even when it looks
    like an option (-o --dry-run, --recurse-submodules only), short flags bundled (-fn) or
    with a glued value (-on is `-o n`, not a dry run). Returns the options in order, spelled
    out as NAME or NAME=VALUE, and the positional words. A prefix of several options is
    left as written: git refuses it."""
    opts, words, i = [], [], 0
    while i < len(args):
        a, i = args[i], i + 1
        if a == "--":
            words += args[i:]
            break
        if a.startswith("--"):
            name, eq, value = a.partition("=")
            hits = [n for n in PUSH_LONG if n.startswith(name)]
            if name not in PUSH_LONG and len(hits) == 1:
                name = hits[0]
            if name in PUSH_VALUED and not eq and i < len(args):
                value, eq, i = args[i], "=", i + 1
            opts.append(name + eq + value)
        elif a.startswith("-") and a != "-":
            for k, ch in enumerate(a[1:], 2):
                if ch == "o":  # -o takes the rest of the word, or the next word
                    value = a[k:]
                    if not value and i < len(args):
                        value, i = args[i], i + 1
                    opts.append("--push-option=" + value)
                    break
                opts.append("-" + ch)
        else:
            words.append(a)
    return opts, words


def dry_run(opts):
    """The last of --dry-run / -n / --no-dry-run wins, as in git."""
    dry = False
    for o in opts:
        if o in ("--dry-run", "-n"):
            dry = True
        elif o == "--no-dry-run":
            dry = False
    return dry


def positionals(args, value_flags):
    out, skip = [], False
    for a in args:
        if skip:
            skip = False
        elif a.startswith("-") and a != "-":
            skip = a in value_flags
        else:
            out.append(a)
    return out


# ---------------------------------------------------------- destinations

def run(args, cwd):
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(args, cwd=cwd, capture_output=True, text=True,
                           errors="replace", timeout=20)
        return r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 else None
    except Exception:
        return None


def git_bytes(args, cwd, stdin=b""):
    """Raw git output within the scan's time budget, or None when git fails."""
    remaining = DEADLINE - time.monotonic()
    if remaining <= 0:
        raise TimeoutError
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", *args], cwd=cwd, input=stdin, capture_output=True,
                           timeout=remaining)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        raise TimeoutError
    except OSError:
        return None
    return r.stdout if r.returncode == 0 else None


def locate(d):
    """(repository root, has a working tree), or (None, False) outside any repository. A bare
    repository, or a .git directory, can push without a working tree."""
    top = git_path(["rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"], d)
    if top:
        return top, True
    return git_path(["rev-parse", "--absolute-git-dir"], d), False


def git_path(args, cwd):
    """A path git prints, only its record-ending newline removed: a folder name may end in a
    space, and trimming it would name a different folder."""
    out = git_bytes(args, cwd)
    return os.fsdecode(out[:-1]) if out and out.endswith(b"\n") else None


def repo_slug(target):
    m = GITHUB_REPO.search(target)
    return f"{m.group(1)}/{m.group(2)}" if m else target


_visibility = {}


def visibility(target, cwd):
    """PUBLIC / PRIVATE / INTERNAL, or None when unresolved. target: an owner/repo, a URL, or
    None for the repository in cwd."""
    key = (repo_slug(target), None) if target else (None, cwd)
    if key not in _visibility:
        extra = [repo_slug(target)] if target else []
        vis = run(["gh", "repo", "view", *extra, "--json", "visibility",
                   "--jq", ".visibility"], cwd)
        _visibility[key] = vis.upper() if vis else None
    return _visibility[key]


def is_public(target, cwd):
    return visibility(target, cwd) in (None, "PUBLIC")  # unresolved -> public


def push_remote(cwd):
    branch = run(["git", "symbolic-ref", "--short", "-q", "HEAD"], cwd)
    keys = ([f"branch.{branch}.pushRemote"] if branch else []) + ["remote.pushDefault"] \
        + ([f"branch.{branch}.remote"] if branch else [])
    for key in keys:
        value = run(["git", "config", "--get", key], cwd)
        if value:
            return value
    return "origin"


def submodule_push(opts, root):
    """on-demand / only when this push also pushes submodules (the last --recurse-submodules=
    or --no-recurse-submodules, or the push.recurseSubmodules setting); None otherwise.
    `check` only verifies."""
    value = None
    for o in opts:
        if o == "--no-recurse-submodules":
            value = "no"
        elif o.startswith("--recurse-submodules="):
            value = o.partition("=")[2]
    if value is None:
        value = run(["git", "config", "--get", "push.recurseSubmodules"], root)
    return value if value in ("on-demand", "only") else None


def gitlinks(root):
    """Paths of the submodules this repository records."""
    raw = git_bytes(["ls-files", "-s", "-z"], root) or b""
    return [entry.partition("\t")[2] for entry in raw.decode("utf-8", "replace").split("\0")
            if entry.startswith("160000 ")]


def remote_has(urls, root):
    """What every push URL already has and this clone also has, asked from the remotes
    themselves (git ls-remote), or None when one cannot be asked. Tracking refs can be stale: a remote rewound elsewhere
    still looks up to date here, and the commits it lost would ship unscanned."""
    have = None
    for url in urls:
        remaining = min(20.0, DEADLINE - time.monotonic())
        if remaining <= 0:
            raise TimeoutError
        try:
            r = subprocess.run(["git", "ls-remote", url], cwd=root, capture_output=True,
                               text=True, errors="replace", timeout=remaining,
                               env={**os.environ, "GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT": "0"})
        except (subprocess.TimeoutExpired, OSError):
            return None
        if r.returncode:
            return None
        shas = {line.split()[0] for line in r.stdout.splitlines() if line.strip()}
        have = shas if have is None else have & shas
    if have:  # only commits present here can be excluded; the rest were never fetched
        found = git_bytes(["cat-file", "--batch-check"], root,
                          "".join(f"{h}\n" for h in sorted(have)).encode())
        if found is None:
            return None
        missing = {ln.split()[0] for ln in found.decode("utf-8", "replace").splitlines()
                   if ln.endswith(" missing")}
        if missing:  # what they sit on top of is unknown: stale tracking refs prove nothing
            UNFETCHED.append(root)
            have -= missing
    return sorted(have or ())


def outgoing_range(root, remote, refspecs=()):
    """The `git log` range a push to `remote` can publish, or None when every push URL is
    private. An unknown remote has no URL and counts as public."""
    is_url = ":" in remote or "/" in remote
    if is_url:
        urls = [remote]
    else:
        out = run(["git", "remote", "get-url", "--push", "--all", remote], root)
        urls = out.splitlines() if out else []
        configured = run(["git", "config", "--get-all", f"remote.{remote}.push"], root)
        refspecs = list(refspecs) + (configured.splitlines() if configured else [])
        if run(["git", "config", "--get", "--bool", f"remote.{remote}.mirror"], root) == "true":
            refspecs.append("--mirror")
    if urls and not any(is_public(url, root) for url in urls):
        return None
    sources = [r.lstrip("+").split(":")[0] for r in refspecs]
    if "--mirror" in refspecs or any("*" in s or re.match(r"refs/(?!heads/|tags/)", s)
                                     for s in sources):
        refs = ["--all"]
    else:  # HEAD: a detached HEAD pushed by name; any other object a refspec names
        refs = ["--branches", "--tags", "HEAD"] + [
            s for s in sources if s and s != "HEAD"
            and run(["git", "rev-parse", "--verify", "-q", s], root)]
    have = remote_has(urls, root) if urls else None
    if have is not None:
        return refs + (["--not", *have] if have else [])
    # The remote could not be asked (the push itself will most likely fail the same way).
    # Tracking refs describe the FETCH repo. Only when every push URL is also a fetch URL do
    # they say what the push target already has.
    fetch = None if is_url else run(["git", "remote", "get-url", "--all", remote], root)
    if urls and fetch and set(urls) <= set(fetch.splitlines()):
        return refs + ["--not", f"--remotes={remote}"]
    return refs


def read_text(path):
    try:
        return Path(path).read_bytes()[:MAX_FILE_BYTES].decode("utf-8", "replace")
    except OSError:
        return ""  # an unreadable body file is reported by the scan that follows


def existing(dirs):
    return [d for d in dirs if os.path.isdir(d)]


# --------------------------------------------------------------- scanning

class Scan:
    def __init__(self, terms):
        self.terms = terms
        self.hits = []          # (source, line, rule)
        self.unscannable = []
        self.unseen = []
        self.done = set()

    def rule(self, text):
        for number, pat in self.terms:
            if pat.search(text):
                return number
        return None

    def lines(self, source, text, skip=None):
        for n, line in enumerate(text.splitlines(), 1):
            if time.monotonic() > DEADLINE:
                raise TimeoutError
            if skip and skip.match(line):
                continue
            number = self.rule(line)
            if number:
                self.hits.append((source, n, number))

    def command_text(self, label, text, d):
        """Text given on the command line. A $(...) in it runs before gh sees the text: `cat
        FILE` or `< FILE` puts a file there (scanned), a heredoc's body is already in the text
        (an unquoted one still runs its own $(...)), and any other command's output, stdin,
        or a shell variable is unseen."""
        self.lines(label, text)
        for script in substitutions(text):
            heredoc = HEREDOC.search(script)
            if heredoc:
                if not re.search(r"['\"\\]", heredoc.group(2)):
                    self.command_text(label, script, d)
                continue
            files = re.fullmatch(r"\s*(?:cat\s|<)([^|;&<>()$`]*)", script)
            if not files:
                self.unseen.append(f"{label} is the output of a command the gate cannot run; "
                                   "write the text out, or put it in a file")
                continue
            names = shlex.split(files.group(1))
            paths = [f for f in names if not f.startswith("-")]
            if "-" in names or not paths:
                self.unseen.append(f"{label} is read from stdin")
            for f in paths:
                self.file(os.path.join(d, os.path.expanduser(f)), f)
        outside = PLACEHOLDER.sub("", text)
        for script in substitutions(text):
            outside = outside.replace(f"$({script})", "").replace(f"`{script}`", "")
        if re.search(r"\$[A-Za-z_{0-9@*#?!]", outside):
            self.unseen.append(f"{label} is built from a shell variable; write the text out, "
                               "or put it in a file")

    def content(self, label, data, skip=None):
        self.lines(label, data.decode("utf-8", "replace"), skip)
        if not data.isascii():  # a legacy-encoded file: its non-ASCII terms only match here
            self.lines(label, data.decode("latin-1"), skip)

    def name(self, text):
        number = self.rule(text)
        if number:
            self.hits.append(("(a file name)", 0, number))

    def shown(self, name):
        return "(a file name)" if self.rule(name) else name

    def file(self, path, label, in_repo=False):
        self.name(label)
        if in_repo and os.path.islink(path):
            self.lines(label, os.readlink(path))  # git publishes the link text, not the target
            return
        if in_repo and (os.path.isdir(path) or not os.path.lexists(path)):
            return  # a submodule (its own repo), or a tracked file deleted / outside a sparse
            #         checkout -- its committed content is covered by the history scan
        try:
            if os.path.getsize(path) > MAX_FILE_BYTES:
                self.unscannable.append(label)
                return
            data = Path(path).read_bytes()
        except OSError:
            self.unscannable.append(label)
            return
        self.content(label, data)

    def objects(self, root, wanted, paths=None):
        """Read git objects whole: wanted is [(object id, label)]. A blob is its full text, a
        commit or tag everything but the author / committer / tagger header lines (identity),
        a tree each name it holds, alone and under the tree's own path (paths: id -> path). Reading objects rather than a patch leaves no crafted line and
        no merge for private text to hide behind. False when git could not read them all."""
        wanted = list(dict.fromkeys(wanted))
        if not wanted:
            return True
        found = git_bytes(["cat-file", "--batch-check=%(objectname) %(objecttype) %(objectsize)"],
                          root, "".join(f"{oid}\n" for oid, _ in wanted).encode())
        found = found.decode("utf-8", "replace").splitlines() if found is not None else []
        if len(found) != len(wanted):
            return False
        read, batch, size = [], [], 0
        for (oid, label), line in zip(wanted, found):
            parts = line.split()
            if len(parts) != 3 or parts[0] != oid:
                return False  # missing: git cannot vouch for what it would send
            kind, length = parts[1], int(parts[2])
            label = {"commit": "(commit messages)",
                     "tag": "(branch and tag names, tag messages)"}.get(kind, label)
            if kind == "blob" and length > MAX_FILE_BYTES:
                self.unscannable.append(label)
                continue
            batch.append((oid, kind, label))
            size += length
            if size > BATCH_BYTES:
                read.append(batch)
                batch, size = [], 0
        for batch in read + [batch]:
            data = git_bytes(["cat-file", "--batch"], root,
                             "".join(f"{oid}\n" for oid, _, _ in batch).encode())
            if data is None:
                return False
            pos = 0
            for oid, kind, label in batch:
                header_end = data.index(b"\n", pos)
                length = int(data[pos:header_end].split()[2])
                body = data[header_end + 1:header_end + 1 + length]
                pos = header_end + 2 + length
                if kind == "tree":  # entries: <mode> <name>\0<raw object id>
                    at, base = 0, (paths or {}).get(oid)
                    while at < len(body):
                        nul = body.index(b"\0", at)
                        name = body[body.index(b" ", at) + 1:nul].decode("utf-8", "replace")
                        self.name(name)
                        if base:
                            self.name(f"{base}/{name}")
                        at = nul + 1 + len(oid) // 2
                elif kind == "blob":
                    self.content(label, body)
                else:  # identity lines live in the header; the message is all content
                    header, _, message = body.partition(b"\n\n")
                    self.content(label, header, IDENTITY)
                    self.content(label, message)
        return True

    def history(self, root, revs):
        """Everything the revisions reach: every commit, tag, tree and blob, with the path each
        was first found at."""
        revs = [r for r in revs if r != "HEAD"
                or run(["git", "rev-parse", "--verify", "-q", "HEAD"], root) is not None]
        listing = git_bytes(["rev-list", "--objects", *revs], root)
        if listing is None:
            return False
        wanted, paths = [], {}
        for line in listing.decode("utf-8", "replace").splitlines():
            oid, _, path = line.partition(" ")
            paths[oid] = path
            wanted.append((oid, f"(unpushed commits) {path}".rstrip()))
        return self.objects(root, wanted, paths)

    def staged(self, root):
        """The index entries that differ from HEAD (all of them before the first commit)."""
        raw = git_bytes(["diff", "--cached", "--raw", "-z", "--no-renames", "--no-abbrev"], root)
        if raw is None:
            return False
        fields = raw.decode("utf-8", "replace").split("\0")
        wanted = []
        for meta, path in zip(fields[0::2], fields[1::2]):
            _, mode, _, oid = meta.split()[:4]
            if mode != "160000" and set(oid) != {"0"}:  # a submodule commit; a deletion
                wanted.append((oid, f"(staged changes) {path}"))
        return self.objects(root, wanted)

    def repo(self, root, worktree, include_ignored, history):
        key = (root, include_ignored, tuple(history))
        if key in self.done:
            return
        self.done.add(key)
        listing = ""
        if worktree:
            listing_args = ["git", "ls-files", "-z", "--cached", "--others"]
            if not include_ignored:
                listing_args.append("--exclude-standard")
            raw = git_bytes(listing_args[1:], root)  # not run(): its strip() eats a leading space
            listing = raw.decode("utf-8", "replace") if raw is not None else None
        ref_scope = [] if "--all" in history else ["refs/heads", "refs/tags"]
        refs = run(["git", "for-each-ref", "--format=%(refname:short)%0a%(contents)",
                    *ref_scope], root)
        git_dir = git_path(["rev-parse", "--git-common-dir"], root)
        if None in (listing, refs, git_dir) or not self.history(root, history) \
                or (worktree and not self.staged(root)):
            self.unseen.append("could not list the files and commits that would ship")
            return
        for rel in sorted(set(filter(None, listing.split("\0")))):
            self.file(os.path.join(root, rel), rel, in_repo=True)
        self.lines("(branch and tag names, tag messages)", refs)
        storage = run(["git", "config", "--get", "lfs.storage"], root)
        lfs = Path(root, git_dir, os.path.expanduser(storage or "lfs"), "objects")
        # ^ uploaded by LFS's own pre-push step; lfs.storage may move it (relative = git dir)
        for obj in sorted(p for p in lfs.rglob("*") if p.is_file()) if lfs.is_dir() else []:
            self.file(str(obj), "(a Git LFS object)")


def evaluate(parsed, scan):
    if parsed.redirected and any(k == "push" for k, _, _ in parsed.ops):
        scan.unseen.append("a push whose repository or remote is set by --git-dir, --work-tree, "
                           "GIT_DIR / GIT_CONFIG*, or -c remote./url./branch./lfs./include.")
    seen = set()  # both comment readings usually find the same publish; check it once
    for kind, dirs, args in parsed.ops:
        if (kind, dirs, tuple(args)) in seen:
            continue
        seen.add((kind, dirs, tuple(args)))
        if kind == "unseen":
            scan.unseen.append(args[0])
            continue
        if dirs is None:
            scan.unseen.append("could not resolve which directory the publish runs in "
                               "(a variable, `cd -`, or popd)")
            continue
        for d in existing(dirs):
            evaluate_one(kind, d, args, parsed, scan)


def evaluate_one(kind, d, args, parsed, scan):
    root, worktree = locate(d)
    if kind == "push":
        if root is None:
            return  # git itself refuses to push outside a repository
        opts, words = push_args(args)
        named = words[:1] or [o[7:] for o in opts if o.startswith("--repo=")][-1:]
        if not named and run(["git", "remote"], root) == "" \
                and push_remote(root) == "origin":
            return  # no remote and no configured destination: git refuses the push
        remote = named[0] if named else push_remote(root)  # a positional beats --repo
        recurse = submodule_push(opts, root)
        if recurse and worktree:  # each submodule is pushed to its own remote
            for path in gitlinks(root):  # each is itself pushed, with its own submodules
                evaluate_one("push", os.path.join(root, path),
                             ["--recurse-submodules=on-demand"], parsed, scan)
            if recurse == "only":
                return  # the superproject itself is not pushed
        # send-pack --stdin reads its refs from stdin, unseen here: count every ref as sent
        refspecs = words[1:] + (["--mirror"] if "--mirror" in opts or "--stdin" in opts
                                else [])
        outgoing = outgoing_range(root, remote, refspecs)
        if outgoing is not None:
            scan.repo(root, worktree, parsed.force_add, outgoing)

    elif kind == "repo-create":
        if "--public" not in args:
            return
        value_flags = {"--source", "-s", "--description", "-d", "--homepage", "-h", "--team",
                       "-t", "--template", "-p", "--remote", "-r", "--gitignore", "-g",
                       "--license", "-l"}
        for text in positionals(args, value_flags) + flag_values(args, "--description", "-d"):
            scan.command_text("(the repository name or description)", text, d)
        for template in flag_values(args, "--template", "-p"):
            if visibility(template, d) != "PUBLIC":  # its files become this repo's files
                scan.unseen.append("a public repository created from a template that is "
                                   "not public")
        for source in flag_values(args, "--source", "-s"):
            src_root, src_worktree = locate(os.path.join(d, source))
            if src_root is None:
                scan.unseen.append("could not resolve the --source repository")
            else:
                scan.repo(src_root, src_worktree, parsed.force_add, ["--all"])

    elif kind == "make-public":
        named = flag_values(args, "--repo", "-R") + positionals(
            args, {"--visibility", "--repo", "-R", "--description", "-d", "--homepage", "-h",
                   "--default-branch", "--add-topic", "--remove-topic"})
        local = run(["gh", "repo", "view", "--json", "nameWithOwner",
                     "--jq", ".nameWithOwner"], d) if root else None
        if named and (local is None or repo_slug(named[0]).lower() != local.lower()):
            scan.unseen.append("a visibility change on a repository other than this one")
        elif root is None:
            scan.unseen.append("could not resolve the repository being made public")
        else:
            scan.repo(root, worktree, parsed.force_add, ["--all"])

    elif kind == "gist":
        if not ({"--public", "-p"} & set(args)):
            return
        for text in flag_values(args, "--desc", "-d"):
            scan.command_text("(the gist description)", text, d)
        files = positionals(args, {"--desc", "-d", "--filename", "-f"})
        if not files or "-" in files:
            scan.unseen.append("a public gist read from stdin")
        for f in files:
            if f != "-":
                scan.file(os.path.join(d, os.path.expanduser(f)), f)

    elif kind == "label-clone":  # labels copied in from another repository
        source = (positionals(args, {"--repo", "-R"}) or [None])[0]
        repo = (flag_values(args, "--repo", "-R") or [None])[0]
        if source and is_public(repo, d) and visibility(source, d) != "PUBLIC":
            scan.unseen.append("labels copied from a repository that is not public")

    elif kind == "gist-edit":  # an existing gist, whose visibility is not looked up
        for text in flag_values(args, "--desc", "-d"):
            scan.command_text("(the gist description)", text, d)
        files = positionals(args, {"--add", "-a", "--desc", "-d", "--filename", "-f", "--remove",
                                   "-r"})[1:] + flag_values(args, "--add", "-a")
        for f in files:
            scan.file(os.path.join(d, os.path.expanduser(f)), f)

    elif kind.startswith("release-"):
        repo = (flag_values(args, "--repo", "-R") or [None])[0]
        if not is_public(repo, d):
            return
        value_flags = {"--notes", "-n", "--title", "-t", "--notes-file", "-F", "--target",
                       "--discussion-category", "--repo", "-R", "--notes-start-tag", "--tag"}
        for text in flag_values(args, "--notes", "-n", "--title", "-t"):
            scan.command_text("(the release notes or title)", text, d)
        for f in flag_values(args, "--notes-file", "-F"):
            if f == "-":
                scan.unseen.append("release notes read from stdin")
            else:
                scan.file(os.path.join(d, os.path.expanduser(f)), f)
        if kind != "release-edit":
            for asset in positionals(args, value_flags)[1:]:
                path, _, label = asset.partition("#")
                scan.command_text("(an asset label)", label, d)
                scan.file(os.path.join(d, os.path.expanduser(path)), path)
        if kind == "release-create" and root and repo is None:
            outgoing = outgoing_range(root, push_remote(root))
            if outgoing is not None:
                scan.repo(root, worktree, parsed.force_add, outgoing)

    elif kind in ("text", "text-anywhere"):  # text-anywhere: its visibility is not looked up
        texts = flag_values(args, "--body", "-b", "--title", "-t", "--subject", "--comment", "-c")
        files = flag_values(args, "--body-file", "-F")
        repo = (flag_values(args, "--repo", "-R") or [None])[0]
        if not (texts or files) or kind == "text" and not is_public(repo, d):
            return
        for text in texts:
            scan.command_text("(the issue, PR or repository text)", text, d)
        for f in files:
            if f == "-":
                scan.unseen.append("an issue or PR body read from stdin")
            else:
                scan.file(os.path.join(d, os.path.expanduser(f)), f)

    elif kind == "api":
        fields = flag_values(args, "-f", "--raw-field", "-F", "--field")
        inputs = flag_values(args, "--input")
        body_files = inputs + [f.partition("=")[2][1:] for f in fields
                               if f.partition("=")[2].startswith("@")]
        joined = " ".join(args) + "".join("\n" + read_text(os.path.join(d, os.path.expanduser(f)))
                                          for f in body_files if f != "-")
        if re.search(r"private\"?\s*[=:]\s*false|visibility\"?\s*[=:]\s*\"?public", joined, re.I):
            scan.unseen.append("a visibility change through `gh api`")
            return
        if "-" in body_files:
            scan.unseen.append("an API body read from stdin")
            return
        method = (flag_values(args, "--method", "-X") or [""])[0].upper()
        if method == "GET" or not (fields or inputs):
            return
        endpoint = (positionals(args, {"-X", "--method", "-f", "--raw-field", "-F", "--field",
                                       "--input", "-H", "--header", "-q", "--jq", "-t",
                                       "--template", "--hostname", "--cache"}) or [""])[0]
        if endpoint == "graphql" and not re.search(r"\bmutation\b", joined):
            return  # a GraphQL read
        target = re.match(r"^/?repos/([^/]+)/([^/?]+)", endpoint)
        if target and not is_public(None if "{" in target.group(0)
                                    else f"{target.group(1)}/{target.group(2)}", d):
            return  # a write to a private repository
        # Any other endpoint (gists, users, orgs, graphql mutations): destination unknown.
        for field in fields:
            value = field.partition("=")[2]
            if value.startswith("@"):
                scan.file(os.path.join(d, os.path.expanduser(value[1:])), value[1:])
            else:
                scan.command_text("(an API field)", value, d)
        for f in inputs:
            scan.file(os.path.join(d, os.path.expanduser(f)), f)


# ------------------------------------------------------------------- main

def load_terms():
    terms = []
    for number, line in enumerate(TERMS_FILE.read_text().splitlines(), 1):
        line = line.strip()
        if line and not line.startswith("#"):
            terms.append((number, re.compile(line, re.IGNORECASE)))
    return terms


def block(reason):
    print(f"BLOCKED: {reason}.\nThis gate cannot confirm the publish is free of private "
          "data, so it refuses it. Review what would be published and run it yourself, or "
          "fix the cause and retry.", file=sys.stderr)
    return 2


def gate(command, cwd):
    parsed = Parsed()
    # Whether # starts a comment depends on the shell (zsh needs interactivecomments), so both
    # readings are walked and every publish either one finds is checked.
    inherited = parsed.gh_env
    for comments in (False, True):
        parsed.pushd, parsed.gh_env = [], inherited  # each reading starts from the shell's own
        parsed.gh_aliases, parsed.gh_alias_opaque = {}, False
        walk(tokenize(command, comments), (os.path.normpath(cwd),), parsed)
    if not parsed.ops:
        return 0
    try:
        terms = load_terms()
    except (OSError, re.error) as exc:
        return block(f"could not load the term file ({type(exc).__name__})")
    if not terms:
        return block("the term file is empty; an empty scan is not a clean scan")
    scan = Scan(terms)
    evaluate(parsed, scan)
    if scan.unseen:
        return block("; ".join(dict.fromkeys(scan.unseen)))
    if scan.unscannable:
        names = [scan.shown(n) for n in scan.unscannable[:10]]
        return block(f"{len(scan.unscannable)} file(s) could not be scanned (unreadable or "
                     f"over {MAX_FILE_BYTES // 1_000_000}MB): " + ", ".join(names))
    hits = list(dict.fromkeys(scan.hits))  # a non-ASCII file is read two ways
    if not hits:
        return 0
    out = [f"BLOCKED: publishing to a PUBLIC destination, and it would carry private data.",
           f"{len(hits)} match(es) -- file:line (term file line number):", ""]
    for source, n, number in hits[:40]:
        out.append(f"  {scan.shown(source)}:{n}  (term file line {number})")
    if len(hits) > 40:
        out.append(f"  ... and {len(hits) - 40} more")
    if UNFETCHED:
        out += ["", "The remote has commits this clone has not fetched, so all history not "
                    "provably on it was scanned. If these matches are already public there, "
                    "`git fetch` and retry."]
    out += ["", "Do not add an exclusion. Remove the content (from any unpushed commit too), "
                "or move it out of the repo and add its path to .gitignore, then retry."]
    print("\n".join(out), file=sys.stderr)
    return 2


def main():
    raw = sys.stdin.read()
    try:
        payload = json.loads(raw)
    except ValueError:
        payload = None
    tool_input = payload.get("tool_input") if isinstance(payload, dict) else None
    command = tool_input.get("command") if isinstance(tool_input, dict) else None
    tool = payload.get("tool_name") if isinstance(payload, dict) else None
    if isinstance(tool, str) and tool not in SHELL_TOOLS:
        return 0
    if not isinstance(tool, str) or not isinstance(command, str):
        # Never block on our own confusion -- unless the text could be a publish.
        return block("hook payload was malformed") if MAYBE_PUBLISH.search(raw) else 0
    if not MAYBE_PUBLISH.search(re.sub(r"\\\n|[\\'\"]", "", command)):  # g'i't, gi\<newline>t
        return 0
    try:
        return gate(command, payload.get("cwd") or os.getcwd())
    except TimeoutError:
        return block(f"the scan ran past its {BUDGET:g}-second budget")
    except Exception as exc:  # exit 1 would let the command through
        return block(f"the gate crashed ({type(exc).__name__})")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
